  "onboarding.sandboxPrompt": "Integrate my trading program with QuantBot monitoring and remote controls. Modify the project directly.\n\n**Repository** [{{repository}}]({{repository}})\n**Sandbox endpoint** `{{testEndpoint}}`\n**Temporary key ID** `{{key}}` · **Expires** {{expires}}\n**Strategy ID** `{{strategyId}}`\n**Production endpoint** `{{endpoint}}`\n\n### Integration\n\nRead the [README]({{docs}}/README.md), [HTTP protocol]({{docs}}/PROTOCOL.md), [integration guide]({{docs}}/docs/INTEGRATION.md), [OpenAPI]({{docs}}/docs/openapi.json), [schemas]({{repository}}/tree/main/docs/schemas), [examples]({{repository}}/tree/main/examples), and [troubleshooting guide]({{docs}}/docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md). Inspect the existing language, HTTP client, lifecycle, data sources, order-entry checks, and risk controls. Run the integration with the main program in a background task or thread. Reuse the existing monitoring and execution paths, and keep network calls off market data, order, and risk-control paths.\n\n### Configuration and credentials\n\nCreate `quantbot.json` and load it automatically at startup. Add it to `.gitignore` and create a `quantbot.example.json` with an empty secret. Preserve any existing production configuration and use an isolated test copy. Keep secrets out of logs and source control.\n\n```json\n{{config}}\n```\n\nAppend Agent API paths such as `/session` to either `/api/agent` or `/api/sandbox/agent`. Use `secret` as the Bearer token. Detect the sandbox endpoint before connecting to a broker. Use simulated data, isolated local entry state, and separate command deduplication and receipts. Sandbox commands must never place, close, or cancel real orders. Preserve normal program behavior when configuration or credentials are absent.\n\n### Monitoring and controls\n\nUse schema version 1 for standard reports and controls. Report available runtime health, accounts, positions, orders, fills, fees, performance, market data, logs, risk events, and custom metrics. Use the server's heartbeat interval and report meaningful changes from existing data. Retry fills with the same IDs. Never invent production observations.\n\nRegister controls the strategy can implement safely. Prioritize read-only status, refresh, and pause/resume of new entries. Keep stops, exits, and local risk checks active. Resuming entries must preserve the local risk lock. For dynamic settings, report the applied value. Ask for my approval before modifying trading parameters, cancellation, position closing, or manual order execution. Explain any unsupported capability.\n\n### Reliability and verification\n\nSend `User-Agent: QuantBot-Integration/1.0`, `Accept: application/json`, `Content-Type: application/json`, and the Bearer secret. Validate TLS and reject redirects. Use timeouts, bounded exponential backoff with jitter, and Retry-After for 429. Distinguish credential, permission, and proxy errors. Redact diagnostic data. Bound the report queue, persist important events and receipts, and preserve IDs and timestamps on retry. Reconcile applied entry state after restart. Persist a command ID before claiming its one-time permit, and reconcile unknown results with actual records.\n\nUse the included sandbox configuration to establish a session, send a heartbeat and a telemetry report, and register a control. Declare `pause_entry`, `resume_entry`, and `refresh` on the executor. Apply the simulated desired entry revision and report it. Claim the simulated refresh permit and send the result. Test resume, reconnect, and parameter reads. All test requests must go to the sandbox.\n\nSummarize the files changed, how to start the program, where to verify integration in QuantBot, the controls implemented, and any changes that require approval. To go live, I will replace only `endpoint` and `secret` with the production values and restart. Do not request my production secret.",
